# The board bundles are generated output: `pnpm build:board` reproduces every
# one of them from `packages/board/`. They are minified — a few lines of many
# thousands of characters each — so git's line-based merge lands every board
# change in the same handful of enormous lines. Two branches touching entirely
# disjoint sources still collide there, and the resulting diff cannot be read,
# let alone resolved.
#
# `-merge` tells git not to blend the two versions. It keeps one side whole and
# reports the conflict, WITHOUT writing conflict markers into the file. The
# artifact therefore stays valid, buildable JavaScript through a conflict, and
# the resolution is to RESTORE FROM THE MERGE BASE, never to commit a rebuild:
# `main` builds and pushes its own bundles after every merge, so a PR's diff
# must carry none of them (`scripts/check-no-bundle-diff.sh`), and a commit
# staging one is refused at commit time too.
#
#     git checkout "$(git merge-base HEAD origin/main)" -- <each conflicting generated path>
#
# `pnpm build:board` is still useful to TEST the result locally — its output
# must not be staged or pushed. If a gate refuses a staged generated path, the
# refusal prints this exact restore command with the paths filled in.
#
# Which side a conflict marker names cannot matter: the merge base's version is
# what both branches already agree on, and `main` rebuilds every bundle from
# source again after the merge lands regardless of which side was kept. Do not
# phrase the resolution as "take ours": under `git merge` ours is the branch
# being merged into, under `git rebase` it is the upstream, and this repo
# rebases routinely.
#
# This is an attribute, not a custom merge driver (`merge=rebuild`), on purpose.
# `.gitattributes` is versioned and travels with the repo; a driver DEFINITION
# lives in each clone's `git config`, so on CI and fresh clones the attribute
# would name a driver that does not exist and git would silently fall back to a
# normal merge. See docs/definition-of-done.md › Resolving a board artifact
# conflict.
#
# The no-bundle-diff gate is what keeps this honest: a generated path left in
# the diff, from either side, fails the PR rather than only a stale one.
#
# THE LIST IS THE BUILD'S OUTPUT, not one file. It named `board-server.mjs`
# alone until 2026-09-06, while `build.mjs` grew to 36 outputs and not one of
# the others arrived here on its own. Measured on one rebase, 2026-09-05:
# the marked file took zero conflict markers, `plot-ask.mjs` took five and
# `plot-registryd.mjs` took three — spliced into generated output that is
# committable, pushable, and not JavaScript.
# `scripts/check-bundle-attributes.sh` now derives this set from
# `build.mjs` and fails when one is missing, because a list nothing checks is a
# list the next author does not know exists.
#
